# Molecular Engineering for Function‐Tailored Interface Modifier in High‐Performance Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Interface modification of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has been widely explored not only to achieve defect passivation but also to facilitate charge transport and stabilize the physical/electrical contact at device interfaces. In this study, [2‐(9H‐carbazol‐9‐yl)ethyl]phosphonic acid (CEPA) is introduced as an interface modifier at the interface of perovskite and the hole transporting material (HTM) layer into n‐i‐p PSCs. CEPA reduces surface traps, manipulates the surface dipole for energy‐level alignment, and induces molecular interaction at the interface of the CEPA‐HTM for enhanced interfacial adhesion energy and good mechanical stability. The power conversion efficiency of interface‐optimized PSC is 23.6% using a 2D/3D perovskite structure, representing the highest efficiency among poly(triarylamine) HTM‐based devices. The encapsulated CEPA‐treated PSCs maintain nearly 90% of their initial efficiency during a damp heat lasting for more than 1530 h and retain their initial efficiency during continuous operation under illumination.

Interface modification of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has been widely explored not only to achieve defect passivation but also to facilitate charge transport and stabilize the physical/electrical contact at device interfaces. In this study, [2-(9H-carbazol-9-yl)ethyl]phosphonic acid (CEPA) is introduced as an interface modifier at the interface of perovskite and the hole transporting material (HTM) layer into n-i-p PSCs. CEPA reduces surface traps, manipulates the surface dipole for energy-level alignment, and induces molecular interaction at the interface of the CEPA-HTM for enhanced interfacial adhesion energy and good mechanical stability. The power conversion efficiency of interface-optimized PSC is $23.6\%$ using a 2D/3D perovskite structure, representing the highest efficiency among poly(triarylamine) HTM-based devices. The encapsulated CEPA-treated PSCs maintain nearly $90\%$ of their initial efficiency during a damp heat lasting for more than $\overline{1530}h$ and retain their initial efficiency during continuous operation under illumination.
## 1. Introduction
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskites are emerging photoactive materials that exhibit tunable bandgaps, high absorption coefficients, low exciton binding energy (BE), and long carrier lifetimes.[1] The power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has increased to $25.7\%$ , comparable to the performance of the single-crystalline silicon solar cells.[2] For the practical usage of PSCs, it is important not only to achieve a high PCE but to ensure long-term stability in various accelerated tests according to protocols set by the international summit on organic photovoltaic stability.[3]
In a PSC with the n-i-p structure, a rational interface design between the perovskite and the hole transporting material (HTM) is one of the key factors governing device performance and operational stability, which are closely related to the electrical/physical contacts, the hydrophobicity of the surface, surface defects/ traps, the carrier dynamics, the interface energy-level alignment, and ion migration. To reduce the trap density of the perovskite layer, prevent recombinations at the interface, and suppress defect migration at the interface under operational conditions, numerous surface treatments of the perovskite layer using a chemically tailored organic passivator have been reported, as summarized in recent reviews.[4,5] Two major approaches have been introduced: the introduction of a Lewis base (e.g., pyridine, phosphate, carbonyl, sulfoxide, and halide) for the passivation of uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and the in situ formation of a 2D perovskite using alkyl (or phenyl) ammonium salts on a 3D perovskite to eliminate defects on the perovskite surface.[6–13] However, 2D/3D heterostructured PSCs are relatively less stable at high temperatures whereas PSCs treated with a Lewis base are comparatively stable under various harsh conditions.[14–16] Thus far, the incorporation of molecular designed organic passivating interface modifiers (IMs) with tailored electric dipole moment into the interface of the perovskite and the HTM and the effects of this modulated interlayer on the interface energy-level alignment to facilitate hole extraction and interfacial adhesion energy to reinforce the mechanical/physical stability have rarely been explored.
Self-assembled monolayers of carboxyl or phosphonic acids (PAs) in organic photovoltaics and PSCs are highly intriguing given their ability to form a dipole interlayer on top of metaloxide layers (e.g., $z\mathrm{nO}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) and to induce a dramatic shift of the electronic band structure at the interface depending on the strength and direction of the net dipole moment.[17,18] PA has $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ and $_\mathrm{P-OH}$ functional groups which readily bind to the surface of metal-oxide layers and usually induce a surface dipole facing the metal oxide layers. Moreover, PA as a Lewis base can bind to the surface of a perovskite layer. PA-based IMs have two parts: a head part with the PA group and a tail part with groups such as alkyl or phenyl groups, for instance. The passivation effect by PA has already been investigated,[19–21] but a comprehensive study of how its rational molecular design affects the dipole moment and the interplay with HTMs has not been conducted.
In this study, a carbazole group that induces favorable surface dipole inversion and causes dipole-induced interaction with other aromatic compounds was introduced as the tail group of PA to improve the performance and stability of PSCs. The most effective energy-level alignment and improved mechanical adhesion property were confirmed when the PA has a carbazole group. Through a perovskite-HTM interfacial analysis, the interaction between the carbazole group and the HTM was confirmed. With the optimized IM and the poly[bis(4- phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA), which acts as an HTM and exhibits high charge mobility and thermal stability, the PCE was improved to $23.6\%$ for a 2D/3D perovskite-based PSC. The interface-optimized PSCs exhibited excellent thermal and operational stability.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The roles of PAs at the perovskite-HTM interface are summarized as follows. First, PAs have $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{P=O}}$ and $_{\mathrm{P-OH}}$ functional groups. The former can act as a Lewis base to stabilize uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ,[15] and the latter can form hydrogen bonds with halide ions to suppress the halide ion migration that may occur under external environmental stress and during the operation of the devices.[19] Second, the orientation of PAs is a key factor determining the surface dipole moment on top of the perovskite surface. Moreover, the attached tail group can alternate the dipole moment and influence the work function (WF) of the IM-treated perovskite surface and the interface energy-level alignment.[22,23] To investigate the roles of the tail group of PAs, [2-(9H-carbazol-9-yl)ethyl]phosphonic acid (CEPA), octylphosphonic acid (OPA), and phenylphosphonic acid (PPA) as IMs are introduced. The formation of a 2D perovskite on a 3D perovskite after applying a coating of PA was not observed, as confirmed by X-ray diffraction patterns (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The morphology of the perovskite surface after the PA treatment also showed no dramatic difference, as confirmed by scanning electron microscope (SEM) images (Figure S2, Supporting Information). Nevertheless, the PA used in this study exists on the surface of the perovskite layer with strong interaction, as confirmed by Fourier-Transform Infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyses (Figures S3 and S4, Supporting Information). These observations are consistent with those in previous works using PA,[15,21] suggesting that all PA surface treatments in this work contributed to defect passivation. Furthermore, the intensity of the steady-state photoluminescence (SSPL) of the PA-treated films was improved compared to that of a control film (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Also, the carrier lifetimes were derived from the time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) decay levels (Figure S6, Supporting Information). The CEPAtreated sample exhibited the longest average carrier lifetime $\left(\tau_{\mathrm{avg}}\right)$ compared to the OPA or PPA-treated samples (Table S1, Supporting Information). From the XPS, SSPL, and TRPL results, it can be confirmed that the PA treatment suppressed non-radiative recombinations and reduced surface defects.
Details about the FT-IR, XPS, and TRPL analyses are explained in the Supporting Information.
The dipole moments $\mathbf{\Omega}(\mu)$ calculated by density functional theory (DFT) are summarized in Figure S7, Supporting Information. The values of the dipole moment of $z$ -axis component $(\mu_{z})$ of OPA, PPA, and CEPA were 0.50, $-1.84_{\i}$ and $-1.05\mathrm{~D~}$ , respectively. The WF of the perovskite surface is reported to be affected by the induced dipole moment of IM along the direction perpendicular to the perovskite surface. The octyl group as the tail part attached to the PA makes a negligible contribution to the net dipole moment of the OPA, whereas the benzene ring of PPA and the carbazole of CEPA have a considerable influence on the change of the net dipole moment of PA-based IMs.
Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) was used to determine the impact of the dipole moment induced by PAbased IMs concerning controlling the energy level of the perovskite surface. The WFs of the surface are obtained from the secondary electron cutoff region (Figure S8a, Supporting Information), and the valence band maxima (VBM) are calculated through the intensity offset at the low-BE region (Figure S8b, Supporting Information). Figure S8c–f, Supporting Information shows the energy levels of the control and PA-treated samples. Because the absorbance offset of the perovskite did not change (Figure S5, Supporting Information), we assumed that the surface also had an identical same bandgap $(1.55\ \mathrm{eV})$ and then derived the conduction band minima (CBM). The WF of the control sample was $4.86~\mathrm{eV}.$ The WF of the OPA-treated perovskite decreased $(4.69\ \mathrm{eV})$ attributed to the dipole change of the perovskite surface. CEPA treatment increased the WF of the surface to $4.93\ \mathrm{eV}$ due to the desirable vertical dipole orientation. Interestingly, the WF of the PPA-treated perovskite decreased (4.81 eV) despite negative $\mu_{\mathrm{z}}$ . In the literature, it was reported that OPA was bound to the methylammonium (MA) lead iodide to form a surface monolayer, whereas PPA had no such effect.[24] Combining these effects, the net surface dipole of the OPA-treated perovskite $(\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{z,OPA\_PVK}})$ is induced to face toward the perovskite layer. In contrast, the strength of the net dipole moment of the PPA-treated perovskite $(\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{z,PPA\_PVK}})$ becomes too weak due to the random arrangement of the PPA. Interestingly, in the CEPA case here (given its stable state on the perovskite surface, which will be described later), the direction of the net surface dipole $(\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{z,CEPA\_PVK}})$ is induced to face toward the HTM layer, opposite to that of the OPA-treated surface. Such molecular orientation in which the IMs are coated on the perovskite surface is depicted in Figure 1a.
When PTAA is deposited onto these different perovskite surfaces, a shift of the vacuum level occurs to match the WF. Accordingly, the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) level also change. Considering the WF of doped PTAA (4.51  eV),[25] the vacuum level of PTAA is downshifted for all samples (Figure S8c–f, Supporting Information). Here, $\Delta_{\mathrm{H}}$ represents the difference between the VBM level $(E_{\mathrm{V}})$ of the perovskite surface and the HOMO energy level $(E_{\mathrm{HOMO}})$ of PTAA (Figure 1b). The $\Delta_{\mathrm{H}}$ values of the control sample and the OPA-, PPA-, and CEPA-treated samples were 0.23, 0.19, 0.19, and $0.12\mathrm{eV_{\iti}}$ , respectively. Increased WF and favorable energy-level alignment therefrom by CEPA treatment led to the most efficient hole transport from perovskite to PTAA among the PA-based candidates. Changes in the CBM levels of perovskites do not significantly affect electron recombinations due to the high LUMO level of PTAA. The trend in the TRPL decay of the perovskite/ PA/PTAA samples (Figure S9 and Table S1, Supporting Information) was identical to the energy-level alignment results between the perovskite and PTAA. Taken together, CEPA is the most effective IM for favorable energy-level alignment.
The perovskite/HTM interface is the most unstable region within the n-i-p device, which causes fractures to occur first when external stresses (heat, moisture, and light) are applied.[26,27] Therefore, an improvement in physical contact is critical for the stabilization of the interface. To investigate the effect of PA surface treatments on the mechanical stability of the perovskite-HTM interface, we performed double cantilever beam (DCB) tests. A sandwiched structure was prepared by attaching an epoxy-coated glass beam to a specimen with the structure shown in Figure 2a. As a result of measuring the fracture energy $(G_{\mathrm{C}})$ at which cracks were generated in the specimen (Figure 2b), the control specimen was found to have a $G_{\mathrm{C}}$ value of $0.77\pm0.28\mathrm{~J~m}^{-2}$ . The CEPA surface-treated specimens showed a remarkably high $G_{\mathrm{C}}$ value of $3.18\pm0.43\mathrm{~J~m}^{-2}$ , whereas the OPA and PPA-treated specimens had values of $0.74\pm0.30$ and $0.83\pm0.08\mathrm{~J~m}^{-2}$ , respectively, similar to that of the control specimen. This observation suggests that the carbazole group of CEPA played a decisive role in enhancing the mechanical adhesion at the interface. All crack propagation occurred at the PTAA-perovskite interface, as confirmed by the color of bare perovskite film and the presence of symmetrically delaminated PTAA layer on the epoxy film after DCB tests (Figure  2c,d, and Figure S10, Supporting Information). For convenience in the upcoming analysis, the glass/compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{c}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)}$ /mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{mp-TiO}_{2}\right)$ /perovskite part is henceforth denoted as the “perovskite side” and the glass/epoxy/ PTAA part is referred to as the “PTAA side.”
Because it was not possible visually to determine where the PA existed after the DCB test, we measured the TRPL of the “perovskite side” before and after the DCB test to derive the location of the PA. Figure  2e,f displays the TRPL results for the “perovskite side” before and after the DCB tests. The TRPL decay was analyzed through the biexponential fit, as described in the Supporting Information, and detailed data on this analysis are summarized in Table S2, Supporting Information. The $\tau_{\mathrm{avg}}$ of the “perovskite side” was shorter than that of the perovskite-only sample (refer to Figure S6, Supporting Information), which is attributed to photogenerated charge extraction toward the charge-selective $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer beneath the perovskite, impeding the radiative decay. A longer $\tau_{\mathrm{avg}}$ was observed in the PA-coated samples, and this trend was highly consistent with the earlier results shown in Figure S6, Supporting Information. After the DCB test, the CEPA-treated specimens of the “perovskite side” showed a shorter $\tau_{\mathrm{avg}}$ than the OPA- or PPA-treated samples. Considering the initial value before the DCB test from Table S2, Supporting Information, the decrease in $\tau_{\mathrm{avg}}$ of the CEPA-treated sample was much larger than those of the other samples. Such an observation can be understood by considering that the CEPA is partly removed from the perovskite surface or the PTAA partly adheres to the CEPA of the perovskite surface. For further analysis, microscopic images of both surfaces after the DCB test were compared (Figure  2g,h,  and Figure S10, Supporting Information). After the DCB test, all “perovskite side” samples had cracks at the grain boundaries. In the images of the “PTAA sides,” rough surfaces were observed, an outcome attributed to the detachment of the PTAA distributed near the grain boundary of the perovskite. Particularly in the CEPA-treated samples, this surface structure showed further development. This was supported by a roughness analysis of the AFM images of the “PTAA sides” (34.6/41.1 nm for the control and CEPA- treated samples). Such a rough surface of the “PTAA sides” after the DCB test implies specific interaction between the CEPA and PTAA. The surface changes after DCB test of the specimens based on the TRPL analysis and microscopic images are depicted in Figure S10c, Supporting Information.
To explore in more detail the specific interaction between the CEPA and PTAA mentioned above, we utilized DFT calculations of the BE between the substances. In the modeling and calculation steps, 2,4,6-trimethyl-N,N-diphenylaniline (TAA), a monomer of PTAA, was considered. When CEPA was on the perovskite surface, interaction between the lone pair at $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ and the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ occurred, and the molecularsurface $\mathrm{BE}_{\mathrm{total}}$ (∑energy of individuals − energy of total structure) value was $1.23\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 3a). When TAA approached the perovskite surface, the $\mathrm{BE}_{\mathrm{total}}$ was $0.25\ \mathrm{\eV}$ (Figure  3b). When TAA approached the CEPA-perovskite structure, the BE varied greatly depending on the overlapping structure of TAA and CEPA. When the trimethylbenzene of TAA and the carbazole group of CEPA closely approached each other, the trimethylbenzene ring and one benzene ring of the carbazole overlapped via $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction within a distance of $3.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and the $\mathrm{BE}_{\mathrm{total}}$ of the structure became $1.37~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure  3c). From this optimized geometry, we newly calculated the BE when TAA approached the PA-perovskite structure, referring to this as $\mathrm{BE_{TAA}}$ (energy of TAA $^+$ energy of PA/perovskite − energy of the total structure). As a result, $\mathrm{BE_{TAA}}$ with CEPA was $0.50\ \mathrm{eV},$ which is much greater than $\mathrm{BE_{TAA}}$ without CEPA $(0.25\ \mathrm{eV};$ note that $\mathrm{BE_{TAA}}$ was identical to $\mathrm{BE}_{\mathrm{total}}$ for the TAA-perovskite structure). On the other hand, in the structure without effective overlapping between the CEPA and TAA (Figure S11, Supporting Information), $\mathrm{BE}_{\mathrm{total}}$ was $-1.26\ \mathrm{eV},$ and BECEPA-TAA was $-2.49\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Such a dramatic change in BE implies that the specific interaction between CEPA and TAA in the optimized arrangement plays a key role in stabilizing CEPA and TAA at the interface, as evidenced by the charge density distribution (CDD) result. Figure  3d,e display side-view and top-view change in CDDs for TAA and CEPA in the TAA-CEPA-perovskite structure, respectively. The red and blue clouds indicate the region where electron density increased and decreased, respectively. For both TAA and CEPA, domains with smaller and larger electron densities were located on nitrogen atoms and the neighboring phenyl groups, respectively. As a result, each local dipole moment was more prominent under optimized arrangement with the opposite direction – from the nitrogen atom toward the linked trimethylphenyl group for TAA and the nitrogen atom toward the adjacent peripheral phenyl groups for CEPA. Taken together, it should be noted that this antiparallel dipole coupling of overlapped $\pi{-}\pi$ stacked aromatic rings attached to nitrogen atoms can favor an antiparallel arrangement of two neighboring pair molecules (CEPA and TAA) at the interface.[28,29] We performed the same calculations for OPA and PPA-passivated perovskite surfaces. The $\mathrm{BE}_{\mathrm{total}}\mathrm{\mathbf{s}}$ for OPA and PPA-attached structures were 1.31 and $0.96\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure S12a,b, Supporting Information). We found out that the passivation ability of uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ is excellent in the order of OPA, CEPA, and PPA. When TAA was introduced in these structures, $\mathrm{BE_{TAA}s}$ for OPA and PPA were 0.16 and $0.43\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure S12c,d, Supporting Information). This result suggests that the octyl chain has no interaction with TAA, whereas the benzene ring in PPA has a similar but weaker interaction than CEPA.
Specifically, Raman spectroscopy was carried out to confirm whether this interaction affects the molecular vibration in an antiparallel arrangement of two adjacent molecules (Figure 3f). For the bare perovskite and CEPA-treated perovskite, only weak $\mathrm{C-N}$ stretching modes originated from ammonium ion at $1100~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were found without any additional signals.[30] When an extremely thin (less than $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) layer of PTAA was coated onto both samples, bands at 1610, 1220, 950, and $650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were additionally identified due to vibrational and bending signals caused by PTAA moieties.[31] For the control sample, a strong peak corresponding to aromatic $\mathrm{C-H}$ out-of-plane bending of PTAA is found at around $780~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , whereas this peak disappeared in the CEPA-treated sample. Such dramatic suppression of this bending can be responsible for the strong interaction between the carbazole of CEPA and the trimethylphenyl group of PTAA. When a thick $(\approx20~\mathrm{nm})$ layer of PTAA was coated onto CEPA-treated perovskite, an intense peak at around $780~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is still shown (Figure S13, Supporting Information). This observation indirectly indicates that this bending is effectively suppressed only at the CEPA-PTAA interface.
Hole-only devices were fabricated to check how much PA reduced the surface defects of the perovskite layer and stabilized the interface between the perovskite and the PTAA. The device structure of the hole-only device was ITO/PTAA/poly(9,9-bis( $3^{\prime}.$ - (N,N-dimethyl)-N-ethylammonium-propyl-2,7-fluorene)-alt-2,7- (9,9-dioctylfluorene))dibromide/perovskite/PA(if exists)/PTAA/ Au. The trap density $(N_{\mathrm{t}})$ of the hole-only devices was derived from the following equation:
$$
N_{\mathrm{t}}=\frac{2\varepsilon_{\mathrm{0}}\varepsilon V_{\mathrm{TFL}}}{e L^{2}}
$$
Where $\ensuremath{\varepsilon}_{0}$ is the vacuum permittivity, $\varepsilon$ is the dielectric constant (46.9 for $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3})$ , $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ is the trap-filled limit voltage determined at the intersection between the ohmic behavior $(V\propto I)$ and the trap-filling behavior $V^{n}\propto I,$ where $n>3$ ), e is the elementary charge, and $L$ is the thickness of the perovskite layer $(\approx450\ \mathrm{nm})$ .[32] Dark current–voltage graphs of the holeonly devices are plotted in Figure S14, Supporting Information. $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ decreased with the PA surface treatment (refer to Table S3, Supporting Information), and $N_{\mathrm{t}}$ was reduced. The adoption of CEPA gave rise to the lowest $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ and the smallest $N_{\mathrm{t}}$ among the PA treatments, which is a synergistic result of defect passivation, energy-level alignment, and interaction with the HTM.
We fabricated the PSCs based on the formamidinium (FA) and MA cation-mixed perovskite and treated them with PAcontaining IMs. The device structure in this study was fluorinedoped tin oxide $\mathrm{(FTO)/c\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}/m p\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}/}$ perovskite/PA/PTAA/ Au (Figure 4a). Representative current density $(J)$ –voltage (V) graphs for the control and the CEPA-, OPA-, and PPA-treated devices are plotted in Figure 4b. The statistics of corresponding performance are summarized in Table 1 and Figure S15, Supporting Information. The control device showed a PCE of
$20.5\%$ under a reverse scan condition, whereas the CEPA-, OPA-, and PPA-treated PSCs exhibited PCE of $21.7\%$ , $21.2\%$ and $21.3\%$ , respectively. The superior performance of the PAtreated PSCs compared to the control device can also be confirmed by measuring the stabilized PCEs using the maximum power point tracking (MPPT) method (inset in Figure  4b): CEPA-, OPA-, and PPA-treated PSCs showed stabilized efficiencies of $21.0\%$ , $19.6\%$ , $20.1\%$ , respectively, while the control device showed the lowest efficiency of $18.6\%$ . In particular, in the CEPA case, the average $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ was enhanced considerably by $0.05{-}0.06\mathrm{~V~}$ according to the scan direction as compared to the control device.
Such an enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ is more apparent than that of the fill factor (FF), while the improvement in the shortcircuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ is not noticeable, as shown in the EQE spectra (Figure S16, Supporting Information). To evaluate which factors are most important for $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ improvement in device performance, we summarized data for control and surface-treated samples in Table S4, Supporting Information. All PAs contributed to the increase in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ owing to the defect passivating ability and reduced $\Delta_{\mathrm{H}}$ . The effect of $G_{\mathrm{C}}$ was not found in this case. To verify this, light intensity-dependent changes of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ were observed (Figure  4c). The relation between light intensity $(P)$ and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ is as follows:
$$
V_{\mathrm{OC}}=V_{\mathrm{OC,0}}+{\frac{n k T}{q}}\mathrm{ln}\left({\frac{P}{P_{0}}}\right)
$$
Where $V_{\mathrm{OC},0}$ and $P_{0}$ are the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and light intensity under 1 Sun irradiation, $n$ is the ideality factor, $k$ is the Boltzmann constant, and $T$ is the temperature. All IM treatments decreased $n$ , suggesting suppression of trap-assisted recombination at the perovskite-HTM interface. Such trend corresponds to the result of trap density calculation of the hole-only devices (see Table S3, Supporting Information).
Next, capacitance–voltage measurements were carried out and Mott–Schottky plots are deduced (Figure  4d). In a Mott– Schottky plot, the built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of the device was obtained from the x-intercept derived by extending the linear region.[33] The CEPA-treated device had a larger $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ than the control device as well as the OPA- and PPA-treated devices, suggesting that the improvement of charge collection ability and the reduction of recombination by minor carriers at the interface occur most effectively. Transient photovoltage (TPV) decay measurements were taken to determine the charge carrier lifetime and understand the carrier recombination dynamics correlated with the PA treatment (Figure  4e). The TPV lifetime was $0.87~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ in the control device but was 1.60, 1.57, $1.29~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ in the CEPA-, OPA-, and PPA-treated devices. Consequently, the enhancement in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and the longer carrier lifetime for the CEPA-treated device could be attributed to the surface passivation by PA and the favorable energy-level alignment of the carbazole-linked PA with an alternating dipole moment.
We optimized the device structure of the CEPA-treated PSC by controlling the electron transporting layer (ETL) and applying anti-reflective (AR) film on top of the device. First, the $\mathrm{\mp{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ layer was modified by Na salt treatment.[34] $J{-}V$ curves with the stabilized PCE of the champion devices are displayed in Figure 4f. The recorded PCE of the CEPA-treated PSC was $22.5\%$ under reverse scan and $20.6\%$ under forward scan conditions. The stabilized PCE of $21.4\%$ was maintained for more than 100 s under MPPT. Next, we explored the combined effect of CEPA with an in situ prepared 2D capping layer on the perovskite surface for better performance. Octylammonium iodide (OAI) as a well-known passivating agent was used to modify the perovskite surface,[35,36] after which CEPA was applied in the same way described above. As confirmed through the grazing incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering pattern, CEPA surface treatment does not destroy the crystallinity of the 2D capping layer $(2\theta=3.6^{\circ})$ in the 2D/3D perovskite as well as the 3D perovskite (Figure S17, Supporting Information). While OAI treatment slightly decreased the WF of the perovskite surface (from 4.86 to $4.79\ \mathrm{eV}$ ), CEPA coating on the 2D perovskite increased the WF $4.88\ \mathrm{eV},$ Figure S18, Supporting Information). Reduced WF can activate halide migration and negatively affects device stability, and CEPA treatment can alleviate this problem.[37] The CEPA-treated PSC employing OAI showed a PCE of $23.3\%$ under reverse scanning with a stabilized PCE of $22.7\%$ (Figure  4f). For comparison, a device treated with only OAI exhibited a PCE of $22.4\%$ under reverse scanning, as shown in Figure S19 and Table S5, Supporting Information. The combined use of CEPA and OAI at the interface of the perovskite and PTAA results in a slight improvement in the FF by enhancing the physical/electrical contact of the perovskite and the PTAA via molecular interaction and reducing the interfacial recombination defects, thereby mitigating the hysteresis behavior and maximizing the device performance. Finally, we replaced the $\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{mp}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL with a $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer that provides appropriate CBM for efficient electron transport.[38] The best PSC employing $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer prepared by chemical bath deposition and OAI/CEPA surface treatment yielded a PCE of $23.6/23.1\%$ under forward scan and MPPT, respectively (Figure  4f). The $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of the PSC was $1.18~\mathrm{V},$ which is comparable to the previously reported values of high-performance PSCs using a perovskite layer of the same composition.[10,39] All detailed statistics of photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 2 and Figure S20, Supporting Information. Through this holistic interface engineering, the device performance was successfully advanced, resulting in the highest PCE among PTAAbased n-i-p devices (refer to Table S6, Supporting Information). To verify the stability of the CEPA-treated PSCs, the thermal and operational stability of the PSCs were evaluated. All of the devices used in the stability evaluation were encapsulated using face-sealing adhesive.[40] The thermal stability of each device was evaluated through thermal cycling (TC) test and a damp heat test. In the TC test, the performance change was evaluated by a cycling process through two extreme conditions (from $-40$ to $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . The environmental condition of the unit cycle during the TC test is summarized in Figure S21a, Supporting Information. For the control device, after the TC test, the PCE decreased from $19.4\%$ to $17.3\%$ (Figure 5a), losing $10.8\%$ of the initial efficiency in this case. On the other hand, in the CEPA, the PCE decreased from $20.1\%$ to $19.2\%$ after the TC test, showing a drop of only $4.5\%$ of the initial efficiency. TC tests were also conducted for OPA and PPA, with the results showing losses of $8.6\%$ and $8.0\%$ compared to the initial efficiency (Figure S21b,c, Supporting Information). These results imply that the CEPA-treated device most effectively suppressed the fatigue-induced degradation against thermal expansion and shrinkage at the interface of the device, most likely due to the enhanced mechanical adhesion at the interface between the perovskite and the PTAA.[41] In the damp heat test, the encapsulated device was stored in an $85~\mathrm{{^circC/85\%}}$ RH environment.
After being stored for $730\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure  5b), the efficiency of the CEPA-treated solar cell was found nearly to match that of the initial efficiency. On the other hand, for the control and PPA-treated devices, the performance decreased by $11\%$ compared to the initial efficiency, and the normalized efficiency of the OPA-treated PSCs decreased by $18\%$ . Unlike the other cases, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF of the CEPA-treated device remained unchanged with no significant losses during the damp heat test (Figure S22, Supporting Information). These results also prove that the interaction between the carbazole group and PTAA, as confirmed by the largest $\mathrm{BE_{TAA}}$ and $G_{\mathrm{C}}$ among the PA-coated samples (Table S4, Supporting Information), is effective even under relatively harsh conditions. To evaluate the long-term thermal stability of the CEPA-treated device further, that is, for more than $1500\mathrm{~h~}$ , in this case, we stored a CEPA-treated PSC for $1530\mathrm{h}$ under $85~\mathrm{^circC}/85\%$ RH conditions and then continued to run the TC test. At the end of the damp heat test, the solar cell retained $90\%$ of its initial efficiency, and after the TC test, the efficiency increased slightly to $94\%$ of the initial efficiency (Figure  5c). The superior stability of our CEPA-treated devices under damp-heat conditions is compared with those of other devices reported in the literature, as summarized in Table S7, Supporting Information. Finally, the long-term operational stability of the PSC was evaluated using the MPPT method, as displayed in Figure  5d. The CEPA-treated PSC underwent an early drop in the PCE and mostly maintained its initial efficiency even after $1000\mathrm{h}$ of light illumination, which was higher than that of the control device which exhibited $95\%$ of its initial efficiency. We confirmed that CEPA-treated perovskite film inhibited the release of iodine from the photolysis of perovskite more effectively than the control film (Figure S23, Supporting Information). These results suggest that the CEPA effectively suppressed the halide ion migration that may occur during device operation through passivation.[42]
## 3. Conclusion
To warrant both efficiency and stability of PSCs, we suggested a rational interface design at the interface of the perovskite and the HTM that would lead to the effective passivation of surface defects, a favorable energy-level alignment for facilitating hole extraction, and robust interfacial contact against an environmental stimulus. These properties could be enabled by a function-tailored IM, CEPA, as used in this work. Through full optimization by adopting CEPA as an IM, the champion cell exhibited a PCE of $23.6\%$ under forward scan and a stabilized PCE of $23.1\%$ under MPPT. Stabilized interface with CEPA and PTAA led to excellent thermal and operational stability for more than $1000\mathrm{h}$ . We expect that our strategy of rational interface design in efficient and stable PSCs will greatly contribute to commercialization in the future.
